---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')
const fig = (n: string) => `${base}/figures/${n}`
const vid = (n: string) => `${base}/videos/${n}`

const authors = [
	{ name: 'Minkyu Kang', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/' },
	{ name: 'JinHo Kim' },
	{ name: 'Eunseop Shin' },
	{ name: 'Dayeon Hwang' },
]

const links = [
	{ label: 'Project Weasel', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/Project-Weasel/' },
	{ label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/MK040412/Mean-Collapse-VLA' },
	{ label: 'Slides', href: 'https://github.com/MK040412/Mean-Collapse-VLA/blob/main/README.md' },
]
---

<Layout
	title='Mean Collapse is a Serious Bottleneck of VLA'
	pageTitle='Mean Collapse in VLA'
	description='When a VLA acts on the latest observation only, visually aliased states collapse the policy to a mean action. A small memory-based steering module on a frozen Qwen3-VL VLA recovers the lost performance — past even the MDP top-view baseline.'
	authors={authors}
	links={links}
	hero={fig('ant_hero.png')}
	heroAlt='Ant Maze rollout — 3rd-person view (left) and top-view (right)'
	faviconHref={`${base}/weasel-logo.png`}
>
	<div class='tldr-box'>
		<h2>TL;DR</h2>
		<p>
			A VLA trained to act on the <b>latest observation only</b> mean-collapses on
			POMDP tasks: when two distinct underlying states share a similar observation,
			the optimal Markovian action is the <b>mean over plausible futures</b> — and the
			mean is often wrong (loops, falling, frozen agents).
		</p>
		<p>
			On <span class='text-tt'>OGBench Ant-Maze</span>, the
			<span class='text-tt'>Top-View</span> agent is essentially solving an MDP through
			a <b>coordinate VQA</b>-style instruction and reproduces the presentation result;
			hiding the top-view collapses success on the <span class='text-tt'>3rd-person</span>
			view. A small <b>memory-based steering module</b> — GLA Linear-SSM + Perceiver,
			injected into a <b>frozen</b> Qwen3-VL VLA — <b>recovers the lost performance</b>
			on the 3rd-person input.
		</p>
		<p>
			The same pathology shows up beyond robotics: a frozen
			<a href='https://github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero'>GELab-Zero-4B-preview</a>
			GUI agent on a Duolingo &ldquo;tap the matching pairs&rdquo; quiz loops because
			solved and fresh tiles look identical and the agent has no memory.
		</p>
	</div>

	<hr />
	<h1>1. Background — MDP vs. POMDP</h1>

	<div class='figure'>
		<img src={fig('mdp_vs_pomdp.png')} alt='MDP vs POMDP graphical models' />
		<span class='caption'>
			MDP agent acts on the state <i>s</i> directly; the POMDP agent maintains a
			belief <i>b</i> from observations <i>o</i>.
		</span>
	</div>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<b>MDP.</b> <i>π</i>(<i>a<sub>t</sub></i> | <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>) — the agent
			sees the task-relevant state directly.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>POMDP.</b> <i>π</i>(<i>a<sub>t</sub></i> | <i>h<sub>t</sub></i>) where
			<i>h<sub>t</sub></i> = (<i>o<sub>0</sub>, a<sub>0</sub>, …, o<sub>t</sub></i>) — only
			noisy/partial observations are available, so the policy must condition on
			history.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<div class='block-statement'>
		A Markovian policy on <i>o<sub>t</sub></i> is suboptimal when
		<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> ↛ <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>.
	</div>

	<hr />
	<h1>2. Most VLA Benchmarks Hide the POMDP</h1>

	<p>
		Most VLA benchmarks are effectively MDPs: the current scene already encodes the
		task-relevant state, so a policy that reads only the latest observation looks
		fine. <span class='text-tt'>LIBERO</span> and <span class='text-tt'>SIMPLER</span>-style
		manipulation are like that — the relevant scene is on screen at every timestep.
	</p>

	<p>
		GUI agents (<span class='text-tt'>UI-TARS</span>-style) are typically deployed the
		same way: each action is decided from a <b>summary plus the current screenshot
		only</b>. Both setups assume <i>π</i>(<i>a<sub>t</sub></i> | <i>o<sub>t</sub></i>)
		suffices — i.e. Markovian on the latest observation.
	</p>

	<hr />
	<h1>3. But Real Tasks Are POMDPs</h1>

	<p>
		Crafting a pickaxe in Minecraft requires the fact &ldquo;I already chopped a
		tree&rdquo; — and that fact is not in the current screen. Inventory, past
		actions, and previously visited rooms are <b>hidden state</b>. Real long-horizon
		tasks (Minecraft, GUI agents, manipulation under occlusion) are POMDPs: the
		policy needs <b>history</b> <i>h<sub>t</sub></i>, not just <i>o<sub>t</sub></i>.
	</p>

	<hr />
	<h1>4. A Controllable POMDP in OGBench Ant-Maze</h1>

	<p>
		We build a controllable POMDP using <a href='https://github.com/seohongpark/ogbench'>OGBench</a>
		Ant-Maze. The agent must reach a fixed pink goal from a fixed start; the only
		thing we vary across settings is the <b>camera given to the policy</b>.
	</p>

	<div class='figure'>
		<img src={fig('two_views.png')} alt='Same world, two views — the source of mean collapse' />
		<span class='caption'>
			<b>Top-view (MDP).</b> <i>o<sub>t</sub></i> = <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>: full
			position, goal, and maze layout — the agent essentially sees the underlying
			state directly. &nbsp; <b>3rd-person view (POMDP).</b>
			<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> ∼ Ω(· | <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>): only what is in front of
			the ant. Visually similar 3rd-person frames can correspond to <b>different</b>
			next steps — the source of mean collapse.
		</span>
	</div>

	<hr />
	<h1>5. Method — Ant VLA, OpenVLA-OFT Style</h1>

	<p>
		The base policy is a Vision-Language-Action model in the
		<a href='https://openvla-oft.github.io/'>OpenVLA-OFT</a> style with a
		<a href='https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21631'>Qwen3-VL 2B</a> backbone. Both the ViT
		and the LLM decode head are fine-tuned with <b>LoRA</b> on Ant-Maze
		demonstrations. Input is an image observation (top-view <i>or</i> 3rd-person), Ant
		proprioception, and a <span class='text-tt'>Go to the point ⟨x, y⟩</span> instruction.
		Output is a <b>parallel-decoded action chunk</b> in OpenVLA-OFT style. We train one
		VLA per camera so that the only difference across settings is observability.
	</p>

	<div class='figure'>
		<img src={fig('ant_vla_training.png')} alt='Ant VLA training pipeline' />
		<span class='caption'>
			Image + proprio + instruction → Qwen3-VL 2B (LoRA on ViT and LLM head) →
			parallel-decoded action chunk (OpenVLA-OFT style).
		</span>
	</div>

	<hr />
	<h1>6. Top-View Works (≈ Coordinate VQA); 3rd-Person Mean-Collapses</h1>

	<p>
		On the <b>top-view</b> input, the trained Qwen3-VL VLA is essentially answering a
		<b>coordinate VQA</b>: given the bird's-eye image plus
		<span class='text-tt'>Go to the point ⟨x, y⟩</span>, it just needs to map pixels to a
		position offset and step toward it. In our runs, the top-view agent reproduces
		the result reported in the presentation — it solves the medium maze reliably.
	</p>

	<p>
		On the <b>3rd-person</b> input the same recipe falls apart. Visually aliased
		corridors collapse the policy to a mean action: the ant walks in place,
		twitches sideways, falls over, or loops between the same two actions in a
		corner. This is the <b>mean-collapse signature</b>.
	</p>

	<h2>6.1 Successful 3rd-person rollouts (occasional)</h2>

	<p>
		3rd-person sometimes wins by sheer exploration — the policy wanders, the pink
		goal happens to come into view, and it dives in. These successes are
		<b>opportunistic</b>, not principled.
	</p>

	<div class='grid-2'>
		<div class='figure'>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('success_00.mp4')}></video>
			<span class='caption-muted'>3rd-person, no memory — opportunistic success.</span>
		</div>
		<div class='figure'>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('success_03.mp4')}></video>
			<span class='caption-muted'>3rd-person, no memory — opportunistic success on a different seed.</span>
		</div>
	</div>

	<h2>6.2 Mean-collapse failure modes</h2>

	<div class='grid-2'>
		<div class='figure'>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('fail_falling.mp4')}></video>
			<span class='caption-muted'>
				Falling-over collapse — the &ldquo;mean&rdquo; action is a degenerate gait;
				the ant tips and resets repeatedly.
			</span>
		</div>
		<div class='figure'>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('fail_loop.mp4')}></video>
			<span class='caption-muted'>
				Looping collapse — the agent re-enters the same aliased corner and cycles
				between the same two actions.
			</span>
		</div>
	</div>

	<h2>6.3 Extreme stuck case — <span class='text-tt'>Mean_Collapse_After_40</span></h2>

	<div class='figure'>
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('mean_collapse_after_40.mp4')}></video>
		<span class='caption-muted'>
			Extreme mean collapse. After ~step 40 the policy stops moving almost entirely
			— the action distribution has collapsed to a near-zero mean and the ant is
			frozen in place for the rest of the rollout.
		</span>
	</div>

	<hr />
	<h1>7. The Fix — A Memory-Based Steering Module on the Frozen VLA</h1>

	<p>
		The fix is a small <b>memory-based steering module</b> bolted onto a
		<b>frozen</b> base VLA. It (i) builds a fixed-size memory <i>Z</i> from past ViT
		features using a <b>GLA Linear-SSM</b> temporal mixer + <b>Perceiver</b>
		compression, then (ii) injects <i>Z</i> back into the frozen ViT at intermediate
		depths and adds a small residual to the action head. The base VLA is never
		re-trained — only the small steering module is learned.
	</p>

	<div class='figure'>
		<img src={fig('steering_module.png')} alt='Steering module added to the frozen VLA' />
		<span class='caption'>
			Learnable steering (peach) builds a fixed-size memory <i>Z</i> from past ViT
			features via a <b>GLA temporal mixer (Linear SSM)</b> + <b>Perceiver
			compression</b>, then injects it into the frozen ViT at L8 / L16 and into the
			action head through gated residuals. The frozen base VLA (green) is never
			re-trained.
		</span>
	</div>

	<div class='block-statement'>
		The steering module is what actually solves mean collapse on the 3rd-person
		POMDP — recovering the performance lost by hiding the top-view.
	</div>

	<h2>7.1 Formulation</h2>

	<p>
		Past ViT features <i>h<sup>(ℓ)</sup><sub>t−k</sub></i> at depths
		ℓ ∈ &#123;8, 16&#125;, k = 1, …, K:
	</p>

	<pre class='codebox'><code>{`(1) Linear SSM mix — fixed-size state, content-gated decay:
    S_k = σ(g_k) ⊙ S_{k-1} + K_k^T V_k
    O_k = Q_k S_k

(2) Compress to m = 32 memory tokens (Perceiver cross-attention):
    Z^(ℓ) = CrossAttn(Q_learn, O_{1:K}^(ℓ))   ∈ R^{32×d}

(3) Gated residual injection into frozen ViT:
    h~_t^(ℓ) = h_t^(ℓ) + σ(α_ℓ) · CrossAttn(h_t^(ℓ), Z^(ℓ))

(4) Residual correction on frozen action head:
    a_t = π_base(o_t)  +  σ(α_res) · Δ(Z^(16))
                  ↑frozen↑`}</code></pre>

	<h2>7.2 Why a State-Space Model?</h2>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<b>Long token sequence.</b> Flattened past is
			<i>T = K · N<sub>patch</sub></i> ∼ 2000 tokens. Quadratic attention is
			<i>O(T²)</i>; a Linear SSM is <i>O(T)</i> with a fixed-size state <i>S</i>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>Content-gated decay.</b> σ(<i>g<sub>t</sub></i>) learns a per-token forget —
			more expressive than RetNet&apos;s fixed γ, more stable than plain linear
			attention.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>Length extrapolation.</b> Train with <i>K</i> ∼ 𝒰[1, 32] + sinusoidal
			temporal PE → robust at <i>K</i> = (64, 128) at inference.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>Identity-init gates.</b> σ(α) = 0 → the module starts as a no-op, recovers
			the base VLA exactly, then learns corrections safely.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<hr />
	<h1>8. Bonus — Same Pathology on a GUI Agent</h1>

	<p>
		The exact same Markovian-on-<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> failure shows up in a real GUI
		agent. <a href='https://github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero'>GELab-Zero-4B-preview</a>,
		a VLM-based agent, runs on a Duolingo &ldquo;tap the matching pairs&rdquo; quiz.
		At every step it receives the current screenshot and emits Thought + Action.
	</p>

	<div class='figure'>
		<img src={fig('gui_agent_case.png')} alt='Testing GELab-Zero on Duolingo matching quiz' />
		<span class='caption'>
			<span class='text-tt'>GELab-Zero-4B-preview</span> on a Duolingo matching quiz.
			At every step the agent only sees the current screenshot.
		</span>
	</div>

	<h2>8.1 The Loop-Case</h2>

	<p>
		Solved pairs turn slightly darker, but solved and fresh tiles look almost
		identical — and the agent has no memory of which ones it already tapped. So it
		re-taps a solved pair, the app rejects it, and the screen returns to the same
		state.
	</p>

	<div class='figure'>
		<img src={fig('gui_loop_case.png')} alt='GUI loop case — C → E → C → E …' />
		<span class='caption'>
			Loop-case definition. The agent re-taps a solved pair, the app rejects it,
			the screen returns to the same state — <i>C → E → C → E …</i>. Same pathology
			as the Ant POMDP: <i>o<sub>t</sub></i> (screenshot) ↛ <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>
			(solved-set).
		</span>
	</div>

	<div class='figure'>
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('gui_rollout.mp4')}></video>
		<span class='caption-muted'>GUI agent rollout (sped up) — getting stuck in the loop.</span>
	</div>

	<hr />
	<h1>9. Conclusion</h1>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<b>Ant Maze</b> and the <b>GUI Loop-Case</b> fail the same way: a Markovian
			policy on <i>o<sub>t</sub></i> loops because the past is invisible — that is
			mean collapse on aliased states.
		</li>
		<li>
			On the top-view input the task degenerates to a <b>coordinate VQA</b>; the
			Qwen3-VL VLA reproduces the presentation result there. On the 3rd-person input
			the same recipe collapses.
		</li>
		<li>
			The fix is <b>memory + memory-based steering on a frozen VLA</b>: a Linear-SSM
			temporal mixer, Perceiver compression, gated residual injection — recovering
			the lost performance without re-training the base policy.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<hr />
	<h1>References</h1>
	<ul>
		<li>
			Qwen3-VL —
			<a href='https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21631'>arxiv.org/abs/2511.21631</a>
		</li>
		<li>
			OpenVLA-OFT —
			<a href='https://openvla-oft.github.io/'>openvla-oft.github.io</a>
		</li>
		<li>
			GELab-Zero —
			<a href='https://github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero'>github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero</a>
		</li>
		<li>
			OGBench —
			<a href='https://seohong.me/projects/ogbench/'>seohong.me/projects/ogbench</a>
		</li>
	</ul>

